playable.evidence:
The rules are complete:
- deck size and contents are given;
- legal options are listed in a fixed order;
- the forced pass at 2 or fewer deck cards guarantees 2 cards are left for the run;
- all three can never pass, because the run triggers at exactly one IN player;
- the PASS card returns to the top;
- scoring is arithmetic with a floor at 0;
- the game ends at 4 or more points or after turn 40.
One minor point: 'skip to (d)' on a forced pass should be read as only PASS being offered, which 'must PASS' already says.
Limits as stated: 16 steps (at most 20), 2 choice steps (at most 4), 15 attributes (at most 20), 3 hidden elements, 1 random draw per round (one shuffle per dungeon), 40 rounds.
Attributes are feasible: points x3; per-player status x3 (IN/OUT plus added encoded together); items x4; leader x1; running strength sums for low, mid and high cards in the dungeon x3. That is about 14-15.
Damage needs no loop: damage = mid + low*(1-torch) + high*(1-spear), plus the 2 top deck cards read with if-ops.
No board, judge or clock is needed. No trading or binding is needed.
Talk is right for 3 players. Players disagree about who should run and how dangerous the pile is. Bluffing about the drawn card can cost a listener who follows it. Talk is simultaneous and comes after the private draw, before the action.
The end at turn 40 creates an endgame (the points leader may stall; the others can force a run by passing) rather than a safe dominant move.
